  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"A project card can record the next small task, likely materials, and intended stopping point without requiring approval of the design.\", \"decision\": \"How should a member prepare a project plan?\", \"goal\": \"Help members make steady progress while sharing tools, work surfaces, materials, and responsibility for the studio.\", \"problem\": \"A neighborhood craft studio serves members working on small personal projects. It has bookable benches, ordinary hand tools, shared consumables, a drying rack, labeled storage, a message board, and a steward during opening hours. Members follow the same basic safety rules. Helpers can support brief activities without taking over the whole room, and each decision concerns a separate routine.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nMembers begin with a small sample and then record the next task, materials, and stopping point on a project card. The plan reflects what the trial reveals about the project’s immediate needs.\nMembers discuss their next task with a partner, who records the materials and stopping point on a project card. Explaining the work aloud reveals missing preparation before either member starts collecting supplies.\nMembers complete a project card before collecting materials and review it when they stop working. The chosen stopping point guides preparation and makes unfinished work easier to resume during a later session."
    }
  ],
